<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Venetian Tinos</h1><p class="st-meta">1390 CE – 1715 CE · capital Tinos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Venetian-ruled island of Tinos, the last Venetian possession in the Aegean, which held out until 1715 and kept a lasting Catholic community.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1390 the last of the Ghisi family, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giorgio-iii-ghisi">Giorgio III</a>, died and left the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-tinos-and-mykonos">Lordship of Tinos and Mykonos</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>. The Republic took over the two islands, first through lessees and later through officials sent from Venice, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinos">Tinos</a> part of its overseas <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stato-da-mar">Stato da Mar</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A Venetian rettore governed from the hilltop fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/exobourgo">Exobourgo</a>, which protected the islanders from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a> and Turkish raids. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinos">Tinos</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, wine and other goods, and a significant part of its population became Catholic, alongside an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> majority. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykonos">Mykonos</a> was lost to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in 1537, but Tinos repelled several attacks.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>At the start of the Ottoman-Venetian war of 1714-1718, a large Ottoman fleet attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tinos">Tinos</a> in 1715 and its governor surrendered. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-passarowitz">Treaty of Passarowitz</a> confirmed Ottoman rule. Tinos still has a substantial Catholic community.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="colony, 1390 CE – 1715 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 64 H80 L70 78 H30 Z"/><path d="M50 64 V18"/><path d="M50 22 L72 56